// Real Formula 1 circuits, rebuilt from where the cars actually drove: the
// OpenF1 API (api.openf1.org) car positions (location, ~3.7 Hz, 0.1 m units)
// of six drivers' clean race laps, 72 laps per circuit (2024 races; 2023 at
// Silverstone and Interlagos, whose 2024 races were wet). Every sample is
// placed by its fraction of the lap (laps start at the real timing line) and
// the pooled samples are Gaussian-smoothed into the real racing line. The
// stored centreline is the one whose racing line (circuit.mjs, default
// settings) follows that real line (0.4-0.6 m rms; Imola 0.9 m, Monaco
// 1.3 m): starting from the real line, each pass moves the centreline by the
// gap between its racing line and the real line; on the gentle sections
// (racing line radius over 300 m) that correction is first averaged over 60
// m (Gaussian sigma), so the road gains no kinks shorter than those the cars
// really drove (unaveraged, the road came out wigglier than the real line,
// e.g. Jeddah's fast sections at a 584 m mean radius against the cars' 807
// m). Rotated and shifted onto the earlier traced layouts
// (bacinger/f1-circuits GeoJSON, MIT License, Copyright (c) 2019-2025
// Tomislav Bacinger) so the lap still starts where they did:
// local east/north metres, points every 10 m in the race direction. The
// rebuilt centrelines come out within 1% of the official lengths (length,
// m), to which createCircuitTrack scales them. No elevation; track widths are
// not in the data. Generated; do not edit by hand.
// timingLine: where the real timing line lies along the lap (m from the
// first point). drs: the DRS zones as [open, close] fractions of the lap from
// that line, from 2024 race telemetry (Interlagos: 2023) speed integrated
